{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>A remembrance of Teru'ah.</b> A remembrance of the verses of Shofarot, to recall for them the binding of Isaac, in place of which a ram was offered. And Scripture did not explain to us what the Teru'ah should be with, whether with a shofar or with trumpets, and likewise the reason for the Teru'ah — why and on what account — it also did not explain, nor did it explain that this day is a day of judgment; and Scripture left it vague with these two words, זכרון תרועה, and relied on the received tradition (kabbalah), for Scripture delivered it only to the Sages. And they received that this Teru'ah is with a shofar, from a gezerah shavah of תרואה-תרועה, the Teru'ah mentioned regarding Jubilee (Leviticus 25:9): ״והעברת שופר תרועה״ — and just as there it is with a shofar, so too here with a shofar. And that it is a day of judgment is hinted in the word זכרון, for it is a term that applies to judgment (Numbers 5:15): ״מזכרת עון״ (a reminder of sin); (Ezekiel 21:28–29): ״והוא מזכיר עון״ (and it brings sin to remembrance). And likewise the term פקידה (visitation) is the attribute of judgment, from the language of (Exodus 20:5): ״פוקד עון אבות״ (visiting the iniquity of fathers). And the Sages, of blessed memory, stated explicitly: פקדונות הרי הן כזכרונות — 'visitations are as remembrances.' It turns out that Scripture explained by way of allusion that this day, the day of judgment, is fit to hear in it the sound of the Teru'ah with a shofar, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, received." "<b>And the reason</b> that this passage comes more sealed than the other passages of the appointed festivals, is in keeping with what is known regarding the matters of our holy Torah — those that are the underpinnings of the faith and its roots — for the more hidden and inward a matter is, the more sealed it is, and the language comes concerning it in brief compass and in few words. Like the matter of the commandment of tefillin, regarding which Scripture said (Exodus 13:9): ״והיה לאות על ידכה ולטוטפות בין עיניך״ — and were it not for the received tradition, we would not grasp the very essence of this commandment, what it is; nor the number of the passages, how many there are; nor would we ascertain from the implication of the verses the specific places on the body designated for them in which it is proper to place them. And likewise the commandment of tzitzit, which the Sages, of blessed memory, said is equivalent to all the commandments in the Torah — we would not know its law of eight threads and five knots were it not for the received tradition of the Sages, of blessed memory, for the language of the verses in this is extremely sealed. And so too here, regarding 'the first of the month,' which is the beginning of the count of ten completed on Yom HaKippurim, there is in it mystery and concealment such that Scripture was right to seal and close it, in the manner of other sealed matters of the Torah — and their sealing is according to the measure of their concealment. And this is the matter: that the remembrance of the world of souls is not spelled out in the Torah except by way of allusions, and its sealing is because of the abundance of its concealment — for the Torah was given to the multitude of Israel, not to the select individuals alone. And the matter of that world — not all the individuals grasp it, what it is, let alone the masses of the people. For just as the fish that grows in water does not know or grasp the nature of fire, because it is its opposite, so too that world, the world of intellect, is the opposite of this material world; and one possessed of matter cannot grasp it so long as he is in matter. Therefore the Torah needed to explain the physical promises that are perceivable by the masses through the sense of sight; and by means of those promises, which are preparations for what a person needs, a person comes to that ultimate end which is the essential intent." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'In the first of the month it shall be for you a solemn rest, a remembrance of Teru'ah, a holy convocation' — באחד לחדש, this is Abraham, of whom it is written (Ezekiel 33:24): אחד היה אברהם; זכרון תרועה, this is Isaac; מקרא קדש, this is Jacob. The intent of this Midrash is that the blasts of Rosh HaShanah allude to the divine attributes. And just as the attribute of awe is above, surrounded by the attribute of mercy — for were it not so, it would destroy and consume the entire world in its flames — so too the Teru'ah is surrounded by a simple blast (teki'ah) before it and a simple blast after it; for had we sounded a Teru'ah alone, it would appear as though this were cutting down the plantings (a heresy, as if severing the attributes from each other). And according to this approach, it is explicit in Scripture that the day of Rosh HaShanah is a day of judgment — from the term תרועה — and it is in mercy — from the term זכרון. If so, then זכרון תרועה is like שופר תרועה, יום תרועה — and this is clear."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:06.301345810Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}